The strain-hardening exponent is an important measure of the strain-hardening capability of a metal. Furthermore, the greater the magnitude of the strain hardening exponent, the greater the strain hardening for a specific amount of plastic strain [ 47 ]. Compared with other metrics, such as flow stress and ultimate strength, the strain hardening exponent provides a more direct reflection of the strain hardening behaviour of materials [ 48 ]. Various empirical equations have been proposed by several scholars to evaluate the strain hardening exponent at various levels. Ludwik [ 49 ], who provided an empirical relationship, was the initiator of the first model, which was proposed in the year 1909. 2 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
